  "body": "The live PayPal event and merchandise-expiry workers can expose exception contents in logs and stop processing if a diagnostic sink fails. Use fixed JSON error events, isolate synchronous diagnostic failures, preserve exactly one tick per interval, and propagate asynchronous cancellation.\n\nThis is the narrow backport of the two existing workers from #393; the provider-expansion stack and its future reconciliation worker remain in that PR. No payment transition, SQL, API contract, provider flag or retry policy changes. The original author is credited. Based on merged #336, which supplies the separate invitation/privacy and logistics-atomicity corrections.\n\nValidation: Stack production/test build and full backend 2,564 examples passed; focused logging 17 examples passed (including 200 generated exception-text inputs); repository quality/formal/release/CI policy passed, zero formal errors; strict catalog audit passed (1,442 files / 1,126 classified candidates); diff check passed. An initial targeted command compiled but failed on test-selector quoting; the correct focused command and full Stack suite subsequently passed. Hosted checks are pending.\n\nThe audit matrix and delivery evidence are in `docs/critical-completion-2026-09-16.md`. No new migration or flag. Prefer roll-forward recovery because reverting reinstates the unsafe logging behavior. Production deployment remains pending the protected merge, immutable image and guarded release preflight; no remote financial mutation or provider sandbox success is claimed.\n\n\nAdditional local deployment qualification: the existing PostgreSQL invitation suite passed 15 examples, and the event-relations database regression passed. The complete 102-migration production-schema rehearsal, real backend boot and idempotent restart passed on a fresh local database. Docker was unresponsive, so only the owned client was stopped; native PostgreSQL was used. The initial migration rehearsal used an unsupported libpq DSN; it migrated successfully but failed at application startup, then passed in full after fixing the fixture URL. No production migration has run.\n\n\n\n## Production delivery evidence \u2014 2026-09-17\n\nDeployed merged revision `9f0da14de84919f8fba391fa752b7df7412a600d` to both Fly `tdf-hq` machines (ord/lax) using `scripts/production-release.mjs` after [merged-revision CI/image](https://github.com/diegueins680/tdf-app/actions/runs/35238806594) succeeded. Verified amd64 digest `sha256:fd7a015858ff34df984a718097afddae22500c21282bfa4434aafeeff39a594d`, per-machine health/version, all 103 migrations and full schema gate, zero release leases, zero private/suppressed public-event rows, and restored discovery/RSVP. Maintenance completed at 15:31:33Z. PayPal intake remained enabled; refunds/Datafast remained disabled. No live payment or invitation mutation was used.\n\nAuthorized browser smoke: health/version200; Admin invitation list200 with78 rows for event86; anonymous list401; logistics200 with expected shape. Worker configuration present; 100-line operational sample had zero worker tick failures/disabled warnings; provider queue empty. Local regression/CI remain the evidence for injected logging failures and transactional concurrency.\n\nC01/C02/C03/C04 meet delivery acceptance. On 2026-09-17 at 20:43:56Z, a newly created synthetic account authenticated through the normal signup/login endpoints with only the Customer role. In an isolated browser context on production revision 9f0da14de84919f8fba391fa752b7df7412a600d, event86 returned200, isOrganizer was false, and its invitation list returned200 with zero rows. The same request without authentication returned401; /admin/users returned403. The original Admin browser session remained intact. No invitation was created or modified, and no password/token/message contents were printed. The application subsequently confirmed the same Customer session. C01 final production acceptance is complete; no critical-scope blocker remains.\n\nThe first release attempt failed schema verification before image changes and temporarily disrupted startup of the original image. Recovery and the exact RSVP/refund-constraint compatibility requirements are documented in `docs/critical-completion-2026-09-16.md`. The corrected release completed without rollback. Required reviews/checks and migration ancestry were respected.\n\n",
